\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar rise not bad for Japan -- Sakakibara quoted. \b0\par\par\uc2 376 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 20 (Reuter) - Eisuke Sakakibara, a senior official at the Japanese Finance Ministry, was on Tuesday quoted as saying that even if the dollar rises above 110 yen, there is only a small chance of such a rise having an adverse effect on the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  If the dollar/yen exchange rate is to settle at around 110 yen, it would not be strange to see the yen weaken a bit more temporarily, Sakakibara, director-general of the Japanese Finance Ministry's International Finance Bureau, said in an interview with the Nikkei Financial Daily. The dollar was standing at around 108 yen in Tuesday's early Tokyo trade. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara was quoted as saying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  takes full effect if it is in line with economic fundamentals, such as when the yen's sharp rise was reversed last summer. \par \par \uc2  The knack of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is to conduct it aggressively when the market is losing direction, and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a large scale can create a flow for the market if it is conducted at turning points, he said. \par \par \uc2  To make \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  succeed, Sakakibara said, it is also necessary for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to get influential market operators on its side. He said he keeps in touch with hedge funds for this purpose. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara has been dubbed "Mr Yen" by the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market because he is seen as the key person behind driving the dollar back up above 100 yen last year from its record low of 79.75 yen in April 1995. \par \par \uc2  In February this year, the central banks of Hong Kong and Singapore reached an agreement with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to carry out \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tokyo's behalf. \par \par \uc2  In April, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  agreed with the monetary authorities of several Asian nations to establish bilateral repurchase pacts using dollar assets to provide liquidity in times of financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said he was thinking about a way of using yen-denominated assets to stabilise the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  For this, he said, it was necessary to eliminate obstacles to foreign operators' investment in yen-denominated assets. He said that the monetary authorities of other Asian nations want well-established systems for Japanese bond settlements and short-term money markets. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds8k047eu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
