\par\par \b \uc2  = \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  View -2: PPI Less Watched Than Jobs Figures \b0\par\par\uc2 408 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 1996\par 23:32\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The bond and stock markets rallied last week after data\par showing slowing U.S. manufacturing and lower-than-expected\par jobs growth for July. The closely watched non-farm payroll\par figures - which have exceeded forecasts for months, roiling\par inflation-conscious bond markets - rose by 193,000 jobs in\par July, compared with market expectations of a 203,000 rise.\par    The benchmark 30-year Treasury closed Friday at 90\par 18/32, up 1 1/8 in price, to yield 6.74%. The Dow Jones\par Industrial Average ended at 5,679.83, up 85.08 points.\par    This week's most important U.S. inflation indicator is\par the July producer price index (PPI), due Friday. It's\par forecast to rise by 0.2% after a 0.2% rise in June.\par    But Kathleen Stephansen, senior economist at Donald,\par Lufkin & Jenrette, Securities Corp., said the market cares\par less about PPI than jobs. "Fear of inflation is much more\par concentrated in the wage component, not in PPI, but in CPI,"\par she said. July's consumer price index appears Aug. 13.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Also supporting the dollar this week is the notion that\par if it slips much more against the yen, the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par might \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stop its fall. On Friday, there were\par unconfirmed rumors the Bank of Japan was buying dollars.\par    But in the background, dollar bears are drawing\par encouragement from the longer-term outlook for German and\par Japanese interest rates, which they insist will have to rise\par eventually from their current low levels, luring investors\par to mark- and yen-denominated assets and out of dollars.\par    "There is upward potential of rates in those countries\par of hundreds of (basis) points," said David DeRosa, a\par director, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at SBC Warburg in New York. "If\par there is a U.S. rate hike, it would be a short run and just\par fine-tuning. If rates rise in Japan and Germany, those two\par countries would be on the start of a rate-rise trend."\par    Japan's official discount rate, at a historically low\par 0.50%, is the rate at which the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lends to\par certain types of banks.\par    However, the next German move before any rise, many\par market-watchers think, could well be a cut in the repo rate,\par at which the Bundesbank does most of its lending to the\par banking system. It's been 3.30% since February.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) DOW JONES NEWS 08-04-96\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1732EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4137 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011013ds8401svv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
