\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar seen falling until central banks' action. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 688 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juli 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 23 (Reuter) - Rattled by fragile stocks on Wall Street and speculation of a rate rise in Japan, the dollar may continue its downward march until major central banks take action, dealers said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The banks may talk up the dollar or possibly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost it if it falls to around the 105 yen level, they said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar plunged to a two-month low of 106.95 yen on Tuesday morning, an abrupt U-turn from the 2-1/2 year high of 111.20 yen set less than two weeks ago. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market expects the dollar to keep falling. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's fall below 106.50 yen will likely make the trend very weak," said Tetsuya Ogura, a dealer at Nippon Credit Bank. \par \par \uc2  Ogura said investors who have been holding long-term long positions may start unloading those positions if the dollar starts falling rapidly below a key support of 106.50 yen. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Japanese exporters are also expected to be eager to sell dollars well above 105 yen. Exporters are believed to have set a target to sell at 105 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's recent upward climb fizzled after it hit a high of 111.20 yen, getting a double blow from plunges in stock prices in New York and growing speculation of an interest rate rise in Japan amid a recovery in the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's recent slide is in reaction to its rally above 110 yen, when everyone was so bullish," said Ryohei Muramatsu, a manager for the treasury and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  division of Commerzbank AG. \par \par \uc2  "I think the dollar will decline to the lower end of 106 yen in the near term and may even decline further on a longer-term basis," Muramatsu added. \par \par \uc2  He said a solid recovery in Japan's economy and a growing view that the U.S. economy may slow down later this year could mean higher rates in Japan and lower rates in the United States, reducing the interest rate gap between the two nations and thereby reducing demand for dollars. \par \par \uc2  "It would be difficult for the U.S. to tighten its credit at this stage because such action might lead traders to believe that the U.S. was suffering inflation," Muramatsu said. \par \par \uc2  A rise in inflationary pressure would push bond yields up by decreasing the value of bonds, which could also undermine the value of U.S. stocks and the dollar, Muramatsu added. \par \par \uc2  Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan is expected to repeat his earlier comments in testimony before a congressional panel later on Tuesday. He said last week that the Fed would tighten credit if needed to head off inflation and keep economic expansion on track. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the technical climate for the dollar turned weak particularly after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  broke below key support of 107.52 yen overnight, which was the mid-point between a low of 103.83 yen and this year's high of 111.20 yen. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said the dollar's decisive fall below the 90-day moving average level of about 107.75 yen on Monday in Tokyo also made it look vulnerable on charts. \par \par \uc2  "The yen buying mood that comes from speculation of a rate hike in Japan has been hurting the dollar recently," said Naoki Ishii, deputy general manager at Mitsui Trust and Banking. \par \par \uc2  Ishii added, however, that there would be dollar-buying demand waiting from Japanese importers and investors, including pension funds, to prevent the U.S. unit from falling too rapidly. \par \par \uc2  "In any case, there seems to be a market consensus that the dollar will move in a range of 105-110 yen," Ishii said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the monetary authorities of the United States, Germany and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would talk up the dollar or possibly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  should it come under heavy selling pressure. \par \par \uc2  "The floor of the dollar will be solid at around 105 yen, as concerns over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are expected to grow at that point," Nippon Credit Bank's Ogura said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds7n0513g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
