\par\par \b \uc2  Yen attack on dollar falters, bourses mixed. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Peter Griffiths \par \uc2 831 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 April 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 30 (Reuter) - A yen attack on the dollar which cut it down by two yen in two days appeared to run out of steam on Tuesday and French stocks bucked a trend of dull and listless trading in Europe to close sharply higher. \par \par \uc2  European bourses were mixed with sentiment dampened by lower early trading on Wall Street, The Dow Industrial Average was down 24 points at one stage but later recovered slightly. \par \par \uc2  London closed 8.7 points up at the top of a narrow trading range in a cautious market, while German shares ended post-bourse trading down, but up from earlier lows. \par \par \uc2  The Paris bourse was most positive, taking heart from new government data indicating the economy showed signs of recovery and closing up three quarters of a percent. \par \par \uc2  In the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market the dollar consolidated well above 104 yen in European afternoon trading but was still seen as vulnerable. The absence of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend it against the yen was causing furrowed brows. \par \par \uc2  In the European afternoon, fears that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would ambush the market on the downside allowed the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to build a base above 104 yen following its two yen fall. \par \par \uc2  But the respite for the dollar was seen as temporary. If the Bank of Japan stayed out of the frame, dealers said, it was only a matter of time before the dollar drifted lower still. \par \par \uc2  The main question circulating in the market was whether the BOJ was comfortable with the dollar at these levels or whether it was lulling the market into a false sense of security. \par \par \uc2  While dollar weakness against the yen was the focus, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmed against the mark to 14-month highs -- pushing strongly above 1.53 mark level. It was boosted by strong U.S. consumer confidence data and speculation the Bundesbank will trim interest rates in the not too distant future, promoting growth rather than worrying about inflation. \par \par \uc2  London stocks closed at their day's high after Monday's losses. But gains had been limited ahead of a raft of U.S. economic data, culminating in jobs figures on Friday and British local elections on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  A main focus was the news that Hanson Plc agreed to sell its 12.5 percent stake in the National Grid electricity transmission network at 192.5 pence per share to HSBC James Capel. \par \par \uc2  A sharp sell-off in stocks and bonds followed stronger than expected U.S. jobs numbers in February and March, heightening market nervousness in the lead up to this week's U.S. figures. \par \par \uc2  "People are fairly nervous ahead of Friday. If the data shows an acceleration or slowdown in the economic growth we've seen up to now, that will have an impact on bond and stock markets," said one senior trader. \par \par \uc2  Heavy losses by the ruling Conservative party at the local elections are largely taken account of but the prospect was doing little for market tone and, in the lead up to a general election, jitters are likely to increase rather than decrease, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  German shares ended post-bourse trade slightly down up from a dip of nearly half a percentage point. Dealers reported low volumes and nervous sentiment ahead of the May Day holiday. They said the market failed to draw support from a stronger  dollar or stronger bonds. \par \par \uc2  The Paris rally came after government figures showed that unemployment stabilised and a survey indicated business leaders had improved their outlook for industrial activity. \par \par \uc2  Private economists said the data underpinned hopes for a rebound in the third quarter although growth was expected to remain anaemic in the first half of the year. \par \par \uc2  The French recovery came despite a slump in the shares of Cie Financiere de Suez, which weighed on the index. Suez closed 3.56 percent lower after it had been suspended on Monday. The financial and industrial holding company has agreed to sell control of its Banque Indosuez unit to Credit Agricole because the low rating of the banking unit raised its borrowing costs. \par \par \uc2  One analyst said that "by selling Indosuez, Cie de Suez is losing the major part of its \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  structure and becomes no more than a big investment fund." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  AT 1600 GMT \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 1.5305 marks, up from Monday evening's 1.5227. It was at 104.54 yen, compared with 104.50 in Europe late Monday \par \par \uc2  STOCKS \par \par \uc2  The Financial Times-Stock Exchange index of 100 leading British shares ended 8.7 points higher at 3,817.9. \par \par \uc2  In Paris, the CAC-40 share index finished up 16.01 at 2,146.79. \par \par \uc2  The 30-share DAX index in Frankfurt closed down 1.21 at 2,505.25. \par \par \uc2  PRECIOUS METALS \par \par \uc2  Gold closed a touch higher at $391.75, up 60 cents. Silver finished firmer at $5.34, up four cents. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds4u08ois\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
