\par\par \b \uc2  Tokyo shares slide, Malaysian stocks hit high. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hilton Shone \par \uc2 2,162 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 April 1996\par 14:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, April 30 (Reuter) - Tokyo shares ended lower in dull trade, but late buying in blue chips pushed Malaysian stocks to their highest close this year and Hong Kong overcame a dull start to end sharply higher. \par \par \uc2  Seoul, Monday's star performer in the region, succumbed to mild profit-taking, as did Taipei. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo shares were hit by the dollar's rapid decline against the yen, and brokers said the market might continue to consolidate in the near future on uncertainty over the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the absence of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The 225-share Nikkei average ended down 193.73 points at 22,041.30, but above its intra-day low of 21,953.48. \par \par \uc2  "The market might continue its adjustment in the near future, though I don't expect a collapse," said Michio Sugita, head of Deutsche Morgan Grenfell's stock trading. \par \par \uc2  In Kuala Kuala Lumpur, the stock exchange's composite index ended up 16.11 points, or 1.37 percent, to 1,189.54. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said positive sentiment and high liquidity would underpin the market in the immediate term. \par \par \uc2  "We saw funds buying up some key stocks. But the market, really, has no news to trade on," said one local house trader. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong stocks were given added momentum in late trade by a rumoured warrant issue on blue chip conglomerate Swire Pacific, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The blue-chip Hang Seng Index surged 128.78 points, or 1.19 percent, to 10,964.53. Turnover was a scant HK$3.67 billion against Monday's adjusted HK$3.02 billion. \par \par \uc2  "There's vague talk of a warrant issue on Swire, and European interests are also seen snapping up laggards as their day begins. The market was pushed sharply higher, although trade remained thin," said Sean Li of Amsteel Securities. \par \par \uc2  Seoul stocks slid on profit-taking after fluctuating in a wide range in active trading. The composite index shed 4.13 points to close at 980.90 after ranging from 978.04 to 991.24. \par \par \uc2  "Profit-taking occurred as expected, but this is likely to be for a short while. Basic fundamentals and sentiment are still strong," said a broker at Ssangyong Investment and Securities. \par \par \uc2  Despite the fall, financial and banking sectors gained 13.52 percent and 2.34 percent, respectively, in very active trading. \par \par \uc2  In Taipei, stocks ended lower as profit-taking gripped non-financial shares after their recent gains. The Weighted Index ended 48.72 points, or 0.78 percent, down at 6,134.28. Turnover was a heavy T$87 billion. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said the market, which rose 1.09 percent on Monday, lacked upward momentum through the session as investors remained uncertain about the index's ability to overcome resistance. \par \par \uc2  Thai stocks rallied late to end higher at the intra-day peak on bargain-hunting in the finance and bank sector, brokers said. The SET index finished up 5.22 points at 1,292.61 on thin 4.28 billion baht trade. \par \par \uc2  Singapore shares closed firmer as slightly more interest from overseas investors helped a range of stocks post small gains. The Straits Times Industrials Index closed 14.11 points higher at 2,415.66 \par \par \uc2  At 1132 GMT, the dollar was worth 104.38/48 yen and 1.5295/00 marks, against its New York close of 104.82/89 yen and 1.5268/78 marks. \par \par \uc2  Crude prices were slightly softer in Asia on limited interest ahead of a holiday in many Asian markets. June Brent was assessed at $19.50/60. On SIMEX it was trading quietly in a $19.50-60 range against a close on the IPE of $19.85 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Gold trading in Hong Kong was affected by bearish sentiment in the palladium and platinum markets. Local bullion ended at US$390.10/60 an ounce against New York's US$390.40/70 close on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Following are the Asian market highlights: \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  TOKYO - Shares in Tokyo closed lower in quiet trade, hit by the dollar's rapid decline against the yen. Brokers said the market might continue its consolidation in the near future amid uncertainties over the dollar in the absence of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The 225-share Nikkei average ended down 193.73 points, or 0.87 percent, at 22,041.30, but above its intra-day low of 21,953.48. Its June futures contract shed 270 to 22,020. \par \par \uc2  "The market might continue its adjustment in the near future, though I don't expect a collapse," said Michio Sugita, head of Deutsche Morgan Grenfell's stock trading. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG - Hong Kong stocks overcame a dull start to end sharply higher, boosted in late trade by a rumoured warrant issue on conglomerate Swire Pacific, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The blue-chip Hang Seng Index surged 128.78 points, or 1.19 percent, to 10,964.53. Turnover was a scant HK$3.67 billion ($470 million) against Monday's adjusted HK$3.02 billion. \par \par \uc2  "There's vague talk of a warrant issue on Swire, and European interests are also seen snapping up laggards as their day begins. The market was pushed sharply higher, although trade remained thin," said Sean Li of Amsteel Securities. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK - Thai stocks rallied late to end the session higher at the intra-day peak on bargain-hunting in the finance and bank sector, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The SET index finished up 5.22 points, or 0.41 percent, at 1,292.61 on thin 4.28 billion baht trade. Gainers topped decliners 168 to 121 with 141 unchanged. \par \par \uc2  "We saw some late bargain-hunting in finance and banks but the fundamentals are still not good," said a United Securities analyst. "Investors are worried about finance firms' first quarter results but there's not a lot of hope for second quarter results if you look at current volume levels (on the SET)." \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY - Blue chips began the new account strong on hectic bids by foreign funds, with the BSE's 30-share index gaining 58.13 points or 1.54 percent to 3823.24, largely on the rise in the pivotal Reliance shares. \par \par \uc2  "We were expecting foreign funds to remain cautious due to the elections but London-based investors are still net buyers," said a trader at a foreign brokerage. "The market doesn't look like it's going to come off substantially this week," he said. \par \par \uc2  Funds have been hectic bidders on Indian bourses and most say they are not worried about the outcome of the poll. A clear picture of polls -- currently under way -- is likely by May 11. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  COLOMBO - Sri Lanka stocks closed barely changed in restrained trade which saw selective institutional buying support turnover, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The Colombo Stock Exchange all share index closed 1.57 points lower at 650.89, on turnover of 15.01 million rupees. \par \par \uc2  "There is very little activity apart from some very selective stock picking," said a broker. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA - Shares ended lower after profit-taking by foreign investors drove the index down, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The composite index fell 1.42 points, or 0.23 percent, to close at 623.91 points after touching a day's high of 627.02 points. \par \par \uc2  Losers led gainers by 56 to 51, with 46 counters unchanged. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said sentiment appeared to remain positive despite an extended fall from a jittery Monday after the sudden death of President Suharto's wife, Tien Suharto. \par \par \uc2  "Foreign players are seen sustaining buying interest but they want to get it at lower levels," one said. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR - Late strong buying in blue chips boosted the key market index to end at its highest level for 1996, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "We saw funds buying up some key stocks. But the market, really, has no news to trade on," said one local house trader. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said continuing positive sentiment amid high liquidity would underpin the market in the immediate term. \par \par \uc2  The Kuala Lumpur Stock Exchange's Composite Index ended up 16.11 points, or 1.37 percent, to 1,189.54. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  MANILA - Shares took a fall amid lacklustre trade as investors took profits ahead of the release of April inflation data due later this week, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  "There are apprehensions over inflation in April and fund managers would rather hold their horses," Helen Alvarez at Citytrust Investment Bank said. \par \par \uc2  The index dropped 22.12 points, or 0.75 percent, to close at 2,937.98 points with 6.93 billion shares worth 2.21 billion pesos changing hands. Only the mining sector chalked up gains. Losers overwhelmed gainers, 72 to 39 while 60 issues were unchanged. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SEOUL - Stocks ended lower on profit-taking after fluctuating in a wide range in active trading, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The composite index shed 4.13 points to provisionally close at 980.90 after ranging from 978.04 to 991.24. \par \par \uc2  "Profit-taking occurred as expected, but this is likely to be for a short while. Basic fundamentals and sentiment are still strong," said a broker at Ssangyong Investment and Securities. \par \par \uc2  The market will be closed on Wednesday for Labour Day. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI - Shanghai's B share market shrugged off worse than expected results of many of its listed companies and briefly touched a 1996 high on hopes of an interest rate cut and an economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  The B share index hit a 1996 intraday high of 53.251 points in early afternoon trade, topping a previous peak of 52.957 reached on January 24 before falling back slightly. It finished the day up 1.66 percent, or 0.851 point, at 51.976. \par \par \uc2  Volume was the highest this year at 31.2 million shares worth $1.1 million. \par \par \uc2  However, the A share market ran out of steam on heavy profit-taking. It tumbled 62.153 points, or 8.07 percent, to 708.43 points after rising 13.43 percent over the previous two trading days. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SHENZHEN - Shenzhen A shares lost ground on profit-taking, while B shares inched up on continued market talk that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  intended to lower interest rates in the near future, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The A share index fell 6.21 points or 3.56 percent to 168.12 on turnover of 4.46 billion yuan versus Monday's 4.66 billion. \par \par \uc2  The B share index gained a minimal 0.95 point to 65.75, with turnover expanding to HK$25.94 million from HK$16.07 million. \par \par \uc2  After the market closed, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced a 0.75 point cut in bank lending rates and a 0.98 point cut on deposits. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said investors had became apprehensive over the advance of the A share index despite disappointing earnings by many listed firms. Some investors also trimmed positions ahead of the Labour Day holiday on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE - Shares ended firmer as slightly more interest from overseas investors helped a range of stocks with small gains, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But volume remained very thin with many dealers staying away from their desks due to public holidays this week. The market was closed on Monday and will shut again on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "It's all low-level stuff, but there's been more interest today from foreign clients," said one dealer. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark 30-share Straits Times Industrials Index closed 14.11 points higher at 2,415.66. The weighted DBS-50 index ended 0.98 higher at 598.05. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY - Australian shares finished a touch lower with few overseas leads other than weaker base metal prices to guide local investors. \par \par \uc2  The All Ordinaries index closed 3.2 points lower at 2,316.8, but well off its low for the session at 2,308.5. \par \par \uc2  The June share price index finished 10 points down at 2,333 and at a 16.2 point premium to the underlying index, almost half the 30 point premium it reached towards the end of last week. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said the market had stalled and appeared to be waiting for news which would trigger another surge to take it to record high levels. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  TAIPEI - Taiwan stocks ended lower as profit-taking gripped non-financial shares after their recent gains. \par \par \uc2  The weighted index ended 48.72 points or 0.78 percent down at 6,134.28. Turnover was a heavy T$87 billion. The market, which rose 1.09 percent on Monday, lacked upward momentum through the session as investors remained uncertain about the index's ability to overcome resistance, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  "There is a strong psychological resistance at the 6,200-point level, and the market wasn't able to break it today," said Tank Hung of Yuan Ta Securities. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  WELLINGTON - The New Zealand share market rebounded off intraday lows to close a touch firmer, primarily on the strength of Carter Holt Harvey, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The NZSE-40 Capital Index finished up 1.05 points at 2,153.18. \par \par \uc2  "The feature of the market is Carter Holt Harvey," said Richard Burton at Cavill White Securities. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds4u08m41\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
