\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ inaction has  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  market scratching its head. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrew Young \par \uc2 668 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 April 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 30 (Reuter) - The absence of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the dollar against the yen is causing furrowed brows in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as players try to work out just what the BOJ's game is. So far, fears that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will ambush the market on the downside have kept the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  steady at around 104.00 after a recent two yen fall. \par \par \uc2  Many market watchers subscribe to this theory while others maintain the BOJ may just be a little more comfortable with these levels now the new Japanese financial year has begun. \par \par \uc2  "The machiavellians think the BOJ has been uncomfortable with the yen near 110 and is concerned about the inflationary process potentially emanating from that," said Nigel Richardson, economist at Yamaichi International in London. "I don't believe that as the yen has been the most crippling factor behind the Japanese economy, it has dogged every sign of recovery and has offset fiscal expansion and monetary expansion," he added. But whatever the view of the Bank of Japan's game-plan for dollar/yen, most players agree that unless the BOJ does actually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  then the dollar will drift lower still. Dealers say the speculative element of the market may be holding off for now but note that this is merely in preparation for another attempt to see how far it can push the BOJ. \par \par \uc2  Many players feel the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not tolerate a dollar decline below 103.00, but the BOJ's tactics have been confounding the market since last week. \par \par \uc2  The recent sharp sell-off in dollar/yen that has seen the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slide from above 106 on Friday has come in tandem with a general yen appreciation. \par \par \uc2  Speculation that Japanese money market rates may be set to rise, which would be a prelude to a hike in the discount rate from 0.50 percent, has prompted investors to close short-yen positions built up since the BOJ cut rates last September. \par \par \uc2  The lack of capital outflows from Japan has also been underpinning the yen's strength. Many players had bet on a flow of money into the dollar and U.S. assets at the start of the fiscal year at the beginning of April. \par \par \uc2  This so-called "wall of money" has so far failed to materialise, with the attraction of a buoyant domestic stock market proving enough for many Japanese investors. \par \par \uc2  "With market sentiment so overwhelmingly negative, it remains to be seen whether the BOJ is playing an extremely clever game or is simply tactically naive," said Nick Parsons, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Banque Paribas. "The charitable interpretation is that it is constructing an elaborate bear trap." \par \par \uc2  This scenario would allow the market to build up large short positions in a relatively illiquid market before coming in with an aggressive round of dollar-buying to squeeze it higher. \par \par \uc2  When the dollar hit lows in February around 103.30 the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively, to an estimated tune of $3 to $5 billion, partly through the Bank of England (BOE). \par \par \uc2  There was talk the BOE \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the BOJ on Friday but the chatter has been treated with scepticism. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think they will want to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  through intermediaries. The BOJ will be looking for the psychological impact of traders being told their counterparty is the BOJ," said Eric Fishwick, senior analyst at IBJ International. \par \par \uc2  Economists said another reason for the BOJ's inactivity might be that it has underestimated the scale of short yen positions built up in recent months where investors have borrowed yen to invest in high-yielding foreign assets. \par \par \uc2  "The truth is probably somewhere between the two positions with the BOJ still signalling through its money market activities a desire to keep liquidity conditions as easy as possible," said Parsons at Banque Paribas. \par \par \uc2  --London newsroom - +44 171 542 6701 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds4u08kwd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
