\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Fears Slow Dollar Drop: World  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  By Laura Cohn \par \uc2 312 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 April 1996\par 22:40\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (AP-Dow Jones)--After falling from Asian trading Monday, the dollar came partly back in New York in anticipation the Bank of Japan may try to prop it up in the next global trading session. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended down against the Deutsche mark and the yen, but recovered more steeply against the mark. Dealers said the rise also came from profit-taking on short dollar-long mark positions. Monday, Tokyo markets were closed for a holiday, thinning yen dealings. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's comeback reversed some of the earlier losses, when the dollar fell versus the yen on concern official Japanese interest rates may rise soon. Anxiety about Japan's rates intensified late last week, after a newspaper report the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might raise them by May. \par \par \uc2  But, late in the day, as the Asia opening approached, traders moved that idea to the back burner, and instead bought dollars and sold yen to position themselves before Tokyo trading resumes. 'The assumption is that (the Bank of Japan) will come in, and that caused some people to buy dollars,' said Kevin Lawrie, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Mellon Bank in Pittsburgh. \par \par \uc2  Market participants believe the Bank of Japan (BOJ) intends to buy dollars to stem the yen's gains, which stifle the profits of Japanese exporters. Also feeding into that idea, late last week, BOJ officials said holidays in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week wouldn't prevent them from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the worldwide \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Late in New York, the dollar was quoted at 1.5268 Deutsche marks, down from 1.5290 marks late Friday in New York. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also quoted at 104.76 yen, down from 105.71 yen. Sterling was trading at $1.5077, down from $1.5123. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 29-04-96 \par \par \uc2  2040GMT \par \par \uc2  1033 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011013ds4t03ll6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
