\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Dollar gains against mark, weak versus yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Leonard Santorelli \par \uc2 787 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 26 (Reuter) - The dollar was pulled in opposing directions on Friday as it strengthened against the mark at the same time as perceptions of higher Japanese rates depressed it against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Most European bourses climbly strongly after dull performances on Thursday, with Paris forging ahead to its highest levels of the year on takeover specualation. \par \par \uc2  But the Frankfurt bourse's DAX index closed ahead only a few points after Chancellor Helmut Kohl warned that Germany needed to tighten its belt to bring recovery, with the economy expected now to only grow 0.75 percent this year. \par \par \uc2  The German \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also trode into the money market on Friday to stop a rapid decline in short-term interest rates, saying the slide could send a wrong signal to financial markets. \par \par \uc2  The move came as the dollar climbed to its highest levels against the mark in 15 months because of hopes of lower German interest rates -- contrasting with the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's poor performance against the Japanese yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar plunged by a yen in Friday trade because of downward pressure exerted by views in financial markets that Japanese interest rates were headed higher. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell overnight to 106.25 in Tokyo, forcing the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tame the yen's rise. It strengthened in early European trade but was down to 105.73 by midafternoon despite talk of further BOJ isupport. \par \par \uc2  Economic data issued on Friday gave further proof that a Japanese recovery is under way, fuelling a view that it is a question of when, not whether, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will stop keeping short-term market interest rates at current low levels. \par \par \uc2  Some economists say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may allow money market rates to edge up in the next few months, but others doubt it. \par \par \uc2  Interest rates are watched closely by foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders because in many cases the lower rates are, the less attractive the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is to investors, and the mark has lost 10 percent of its value against the dollar since January. \par \par \uc2  But against the mark, the dollar stayed near its highest levels in almost 15 months, buoyed up by remarks of a senior German \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official. \par \par \uc2  Bundesbank council member Franz-Christoph Zeitler said on Thursday the mark was no longer overvalued against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but remained overvalued against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The mark was also hurt by Zeitler's suggestions that Germany could lower interest rates. Germany just last week cut key short-term interest rates by half a point in a bid to revive its ailing economy. \par \par \uc2  In a bid to stop the rate slide, the Bundesbank stepped in to mop up an excess of liquidity by offering banks a 3.10 percent return on their funds instead of the three percent rate available in the market. \par \par \uc2  Zeitler's original comments sent the dollar to 1.5350 marks in New York, its highest level since February 8, 1995. By midafternoon in Europe, it was at 1.5321, compared with 1.5287 at the close on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  European equity markets perked up somewhat after desultory trading on Thursday. London climbed 0.5 percent, helped by continuing bid speculation. The FTSE 100 index was up 10.9 points at 3,830.2. \par \par \uc2  Friday's favourite bid targets were electricity company London Electric, which put on 16p to 838p on more talk that a U.S. company may bid next week, and Standard Chartered bank, up 15p to 635p. \par \par \uc2  "Bid rumours are holding the market where it is but we're unlikely to go any further unless something actually happens soon," said one dealer. \par \par \uc2  French stocks put on the strongest performance, jumping 1.25 percent by early afternoon. Traders said the bourse was benefiting from  positive U.S. and European bond markets. \par \par \uc2  The CAC 40 index soared to 2143.17 points, eclipsing the year's previous trading record of 2127.63 as takeover speculation and as well as rising bond prices reinforced the bullish mood. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  AT 1400 GMT \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 1.5321 marks versus 1.5287 late on Thursday, and at 105.73 yen versus 106.45. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS AT 1400 GMT \par \par \uc2  The Financial Times-Stock Exchange index of 100 leading British shares was up 17.9 points at 3,837.2 \par \par \uc2  In Paris, the CAC-40 share index was up 24.60 points, at 2143.17. \par \par \uc2  The 30-share DAX index in Frankfurt ended up 4.76 points at 2,537.17. \par \par \uc2  PRECIOUS METALS \par \par \uc2  Gold was fixed at $393.10 an ounce, compared with Thursday's close of $384.25. Silver was unchanged at $5.38. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds4q08tj6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
