\par\par \b \uc2  G7 likely to welcome  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  moves - Japan official. \b0\par\par\uc2 527 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, April 20 (Reuter) - The Group of Seven (G7) nations are likely to welcome recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  developments at their meeting in Washington on Sunday, but \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not be an important issue for discussion, a senior Japanese government official told reporters on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  The official travelling with Finance Minister Wataru Kubo said \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been stabilising since last April's G7 pact to seek "an orderly reversal" of the weak dollar. \par \par \uc2  "While welcoming recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  developments, the G7 nations are likely to reaffirm the importance of maintaining close cooperation to ensure stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has so far rebounded robustly to above 107 yen from its post-war low of 79.75 yen on April 19 last year, due partly to aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ). \par \par \uc2  "At present, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  look unlikely to be an important issue for discussion," the Japanese official said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese delegations, including Kubo, arrived in the United States on Saturday to attend the G7 meeting and a series of International Monetary Fund and World Bank meetings next week. \par \par \uc2  Kubo planned to have bilateral talks with U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin on Sunday morning aimed at laying the groundwork for the G7 meeting, the official said. \par \par \uc2  Kubo would explain to Rubin and other G7 counterparts that the Japanese government is putting priority on securing an economic recovery initiated by private-sector demand, despite its severe budgetary situation, the official said. \par \par \uc2  "Bearing the nation's severe budgetary situation in mind, we would like to make much of economic policy coordination with other nations," the government official said. \par \par \uc2  The official admitted that other G7 nations hope to see Japan's economic recovery led by domestic demand and that there were also some expectations concerning Japan's monetary and fiscal policies. He did not elaborate. \par \par \uc2  "At a time when Japan's recovery has become outstanding in the midst of slowing economies in other G7 nations, it is natural for the United States and other nations to hope Japan's economic recovery will be fully secured," he said. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether Japan's present accommodative credit stance would be maintained to ensure economic recovery, the official said discount rate policy should be up to the Bank of Japan (BOJ). "The BOJ governor (Yasuo Matsushita) recently said in parliament that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy would be geared to secure economic recovery, I think this stance is quite appropriate." \par \par \uc2  Kubo would explain to Rubin and to a meeting of G7 top economic policymakers that Japan's economy was now gradually recovering and that its current account surplus had been declining rapidly due to expanding imports, the official said. \par \par \uc2  "I think these conditions will be welcomed by the United States. I think neither (Japan or the U.S.) has any particular demand to each other on the economic front," he added. \par \par \uc2  On bilateral insurance talks, the official said Japan and the United States had already agreed to seek a conclusion by June 1. "We believe constructive discussions are now under way and this isn't something which will end in a catastrophe." \par