\par\par \b \uc2  Asian monetary officials agree need to cooperate. \b0\par\par\uc2  By George Nishiyama \par \uc2 577 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 5 (Reuter) - Monetary authorities from 10 Asian economies called at a symposium in Tokyo on Friday for further cooperation to bring about stability in the region's financial markets. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and finance ministry officials agreed that cooperation was the key to Asian development. \par \par \uc2  "Cooperation among monetary authorities allows them to conduct policy more smoothly," said Yeo Lian Sim, director of the international department at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "We would like to see the role of this kind of meeting enhanced and more cooperation of monetary authorities and central banks to come out," added Akira Nagashima, deputy governor for international relations at the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  But the participants, from Australia, China, Hong Kong, Indonesia, South Korea, Malaysia, the Philippines, Singapore, Thailand and Japan, fell short of coming up with any fresh measures to achieve the goal. \par \par \uc2  Japan said it would "not be reluctant" to sign a so-called repo pact with other Asian nations on stabilising \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, but fell short of making any firm commitment. "We are not reluctant, but we are studying the situation," Takatoshi Kato, vice minister of finance for international affairs at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  In November 1995, authorities of Australia, Hong Kong, Indonesia, Malaysia and Thailand signed a series of bilateral securities repurchase accords, or repo pacts, allowing central banks to borrow U.S. government securities from each other, bolstering foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and mobilising them to protect their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan's Nagashima also told the symposium: "We are exploring the possibility of joining a similar arrangement with Asian central banks and monetary authorities, but this has not been decided yet." \par \par \uc2  More bilateral repo agreements have been made by Asian nations recently but Japan has yet to conclude one. Japan's Finance Ministry announced in February, however, that Japan, Hong Kong and Singapore had reached an agreement which allows the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ask its Asian counterparts to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in dollar-yen trading on its behalf. \par \par \uc2  Some officials attending the symposium also said the yen may come to play a larger role in the region in the future. \par \par \uc2  "There may be an increased use of the yen in the coming years to match the composition of assets and liabilities (of Asian nations)," said Ian Macfarlane, deputy governor at the Reserve Bank of Australia. \par \par \uc2  But he added that such a move would tend to push up the value of the yen and said Japan must further liberalise imports and encourage its outflow of investments to avoid that. \par \par \uc2  Myoung Ho Shin, former deputy minister for international affairs at South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy, also said officials in Tokyo were determined to stabilise the yen. \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese government has a strong will to stabilise the yen and to develop a transparent financial market," he said. \par \par \uc2  Nagashima also said both Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and its Finance Ministry will aim to revitalise the Tokyo market. \par \par \uc2  "It will have a beneficial effect on other Asian markets. It'll be a 'win-win' situation for all," he said. \par \par \uc2  Although Southeast Asian nations were adversely affected by the yen's sharp rise of late 1994 to early 1995, the real impetus for regional cooperation has been the Mexican financial crisis of around the same time, which caused many investors to believe emerging nations in Southeast Asia could be next. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds4809khs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
