\par\par \b \uc2  APEC Meeting Not Seen Moving Markets In Near Term \b0\par\par\uc2  By Brian Fowler \par \uc2 282 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1996\par 07:38\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (AP-Dow Jones)--A meeting this weekend between finance ministers of the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) grouping likely won't have an immediate impact on financial markets, but could promote stability in the region in the long term, Tokyo-based economists and analysts said Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  APEC government officials have said the finance ministers, scheduled to meet in Kyoto March 16 and 17, will discuss an array of issues including cooperation in the global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, promoting private investment flows within the region and encouraging stability in the capital markets. \par \par \uc2  The ministers also are expected to touch on the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's proposal for an Asian version of the Bank for International Settlements (BIS). \par \par \uc2  In addition, most countries are planning to hold a number of bilateral discussions. Among the most closely scrunitized by markets will be a meeting between Japanese Finance Minister Wataru Kubo and U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin, set for Saturday. \par \par \uc2  But speculation that the U.S. may show only lukewarm support for a stronger dollar has led some to downplay the potential impact of the APEC meeting on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  U.S. officials have steered clear of making any overt gestures that would drive the dollar higher from current levels between 105.00 and 106.00 yen - even when the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently to support the dollar. Some \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts speculate that the U.S. administration is wary of boosting the dollar too much, as doing so might sap support for President Bill Clinton from the auto industry. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 13-03-96 \par \par \uc2  0638GMT \par \par \uc2  1149 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011013ds3d028lk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
