\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar - Profit-Taking -2-: Low Japan Rates Benefit Dollar \b0\par\par\uc2 453 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1996\par 13:25\par \uc2  Dow Jones News Service \par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Next, the release of German unemployment figures indicated that while growth is suffering, the economy may not be quite as dire as some expected. \par \par \uc2  The figures showed the number of unemployed across the country rising by 107,000, well above the 59,000 rise in January but below the worst forecasts for a 165,000 increase. Some economists had expected bad weather at the start of the year to cause large layoffs in the construction industry. \par \par \uc2  The dollar recently was up at 1.4785 Deutsche marks from 1.5744 marks late yesterday in New York. At one stage it slipped as low as 1.4730 marks. It advanced slightly to 105.30 yen from 105.24 yen. \par \par \uc2  Some expect a high unemployment number to raise pressure on the German \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut rates soon. But, said Deutsche Morgan Grenfell's Haigh, the Bundesbank is more likely to argue that the rise in the number of jobless is structural phenomenon and a decision on rates will have to wait for proof that M3 is falling back toward its range. \par \par \uc2  Haigh forecast that although the dollar might rise to test resistance at 1.4920 marks if Friday's U.S. data are positive, he still doesn't think ''the fundamental environment justifies a break at this stage.'' \par \par \uc2  Some analysts warned, however, that any upward break for the dollar may not come against the mark but against the yen. At the moment, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to trade in a narrow range against the yen, supported on one side by the threat of more Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and capped on the other by suggestions that Japanese interest rates have begun turning up again and Japanese banks will be repatriating funds before the end of the financial year. \par \par \uc2  Some see the Japanese government's bad-loan bailout package as a ticking time bomb that could eventually hurt the yen. ''It has the potential to blow up at any time,'' said Lehman's Fox. \par \par \uc2  He said that while some fear banks will be forced to repatriate more funds from abroad to help finance the package, that is unlikely to have any real impact. \par \par \uc2  ''Japanese banks don't have that many foreign assets to bring back home. It is much less significant than people think,'' he said. \par \par \uc2  In the meantime, Haigh said, the Bank of Japan will try to ensure the package doesn't unravel by keeping interest rates low. ''The Japanese will do their utmost to ensure there isn't a meltdown,'' he said, noting that this should continue to keep rates positive for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  (END) DOW JONES NEWS 03-06-96 \par \par \uc2  7:25 AM \par \par \uc2 Dokument dj00000020011013ds3601bf4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
