\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr Mixed After  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Support: World  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  By Rebecca H. Patterson \par \uc2 339 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Februar 1996\par 22:38\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (AP-Dow Jones)--The dollar came almost full circle Tuesday, drifting down in late Asia and early Europe trading only to bounce back in New York on reports of central banks buying dollars for yen. \par \par \uc2  At the end of the global trading day, the dollar was up against the Deutsche mark, but down slightly against the yen and pound, its movements largely guided by reports of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  first entered the market late in Asian trading, buying dollars around 104.00 yen. The dollar picked up momentarily, then continued its slide to an intraday low of 103.40 yen at 1256 GMT, late in Europe. \par \par \uc2  That level was the lowest for the dollar against the yen since Jan. 2 when it hit 103.15 yen. The dollar then bounced back on unconfirmed reports that the Bank of England was buying dollars on behalf of the Bank of Japan. The Bank of England wouldn't comment on the rumors. \par \par \uc2  The dollar subsequently jumped to an intraday high of 104.78 yen around 1452 GMT. That forced many traders to cover their short positions in the dollar, leaving them wary of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the remainder of the day. \par \par \uc2  Though the dollar ended mixed, traders said this was the first day since the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  began \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week that they didn't feel they could push the dollar back down with impunity. \par \par \uc2  'The market was shaken up today in dollar-yen,' said Debra Larsen, an assistant vice president at Commerzbank in New York. 'Traders will be quick to cover shorts now.' \par \par \uc2  Late in New York, the dollar was quoted at 1.4525 Deutsche marks, up from 1.4494 marks late Monday in New York. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also quoted at 104.23 yen, down from 104.32 yen. Sterling was trading at $1.5415, up from $1.5410. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 27-02-96 \par \par \uc2  2138GMT \par \par \uc2  1033 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011013ds2r01peb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
