\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore helps Japan, but dollar outlook gloomy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By George Nishiyama \par \uc2 612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Februar 1996\par 14:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Reuter) - The Bank of Japan held the dollar above 105 yen in late Tokyo on Friday, helped by Singapore for the first time under a new Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pact, but they were virtually the only buyers and the greenback stayed vulnerable, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Although the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are sucking up dollar long positions held by U.S. funds, we are still far from seeing any aggressive buyers of dollars," said Ichiro Ikeda, vice president at Goldman Sachs International Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS) bought dollars at 104.50-60 yen and above on behalf of the Bank of Japan (BOJ) in late Asian trade, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said. \par \par \uc2  The move was in line with a pact, unveiled on Tuesday, allowing the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ask counterparts in Singapore and Hong Kong to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's behalf. \par \par \uc2  An official at the BOJ said it had been in close contact with other central banks on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, but declined further comment on the apparent MAS buying. A MAS spokesman also declined to comment. \par \par \uc2  Despite the apparent Singaporean support, the dollar stayed stuck at just above 105 yen, after closing at about 104.98 yen in New York on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the BOJ bought dollars as the greenback fell, but the efforts appeared aimed at slowing the decline rather than at turning the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  around. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the BOJ was eager to prevent a slide of the dollar as a fall below 100 yen would undermine prospects for Japan's fragile economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ would step in to prevent stop-loss sales from emerging, but once such sales did emerge, they would let the sales build up and then come out again at a lower level, so the dollar falls, but gradually," said a U.S. bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Selling pressure came largely from U.S. funds and possibly some trust banks, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. hedge funds, which until recently borrowed low-cost yen and then invested the funds in higher-yielding instruments, including U.S. stocks and bonds, are widely believed to have begun to review their strategy. \par \par \uc2  The recent rise in Japanese money market interest rates had forced them to buy back yen to repay the funds, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  While the BOJ buying did not boost the dollar, it did make dealers cautious about selling the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Fears of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by major nations was also likely to limit further dollar slides, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Rumours in New York on Thursday that the Bank of England and the U.S. Federal Reserve had entered the market briefly bolstered the greenback in New York trade. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Finance Minister Wataru Kubo said on Friday that measures, such as \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and cooperative action among major nations, were being taken to prevent the dollar from falling. "We are still in a long-term trend of an orderly reversal of the dollar," Kubo said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rebounded in the second half of last year after falling to a post-World War Two low of 79.75 yen in April 1995. \par \par \uc2  Goldman's Ikeda said the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely show their effect later next week as most of the dollar long positions will have been squared, but others were doubtful. \par \par \uc2  "U.S. funds are stepping up their dollar sales on the back of a weak Treasury market. Even if the dollar should rise, it would be capped at 106 yen. If it should fall below 103 yen, it may dip to the 101 yen level," said Hank Note, chief dealer with Sumitomo Bank. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011019ds2n0168j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
