\par\par \b \uc2  Hectic moves by Japan aim to shore up dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tatsuo Ito \par \uc2 679 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Februar 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 22 (Reuter) - A flood of dollar sales by U.S. funds, whose activities have rattled global financial markets, have forced the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) to use aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to prop up the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dealers estimated that BOJ buying on Thursday and the previous two days combined totalled about $6 billion or $7 billion. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's strong stance has made many private operators cautious about buying dollars and raised questions about why the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to support the dollar so keenly at the current exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not aimed at guiding the dollar higher to a specific level, but to prevent it from sliding, because a fall below 100 yen would undermine prospects for the country's economic recovery," said Ichiro Ikeda, vice-president at Goldman Sachs Ltd. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was apparently aimed at keeping the dollar above 105 yen, much higher than the level of just below 100 yen when the BOJ last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in October. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the BOJ appeared to have used electronic broking systems in its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Tuesday, something it has not done often in the past. \par \par \uc2  Even so, the dollar was on the verge of slipping below 105 yen in late Tokyo trade on Thursday, against to a recent high of 106.60 yen on Tuesday when Japan's Finance Ministry announced it had signed a pact with Singapore and Hong Kong for them to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Tokyo's behalf. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said fears about a possible dollar tumble ahead of Japan's fiscal book closing at the end of March and concerns about further sell-offs by U.S. hedge funds were behind the BOJ's aggressiveness. \par \par \uc2  The dollar tends to sag ahead of the book closings as firms repatriate or liquidate foreign assets to book profits or limit losses. This was a factor in the dollar's tumble to a post-war low of 79.75 yen on April 19, 1995. \par \par \uc2  Recent plunges in financial markets ranging from the United States to South Africa were thought to be responsible for dollar sales by speculators such as U.S. hedge funds. \par \par \uc2  "Triggered by concerns about South Africa and falls in U.S. stocks and bonds (on Tuesday), U.S. hedge funds have started unwinding dollar long positions," said Taisuke Tanaka, a market strategist at Credit Suisse in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The South African rand started falling last week on rumours about President Nelson Mandela's health and talk of a quick easing of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls. \par \par \uc2  "In order to counter these unexpected moves, monetary authorities are now trying to help U.S. funds liquidate their stale long positions," Tanaka added. \par \par \uc2  U.S. hedge funds had borrowed low-cost yen or Swiss francs and then invested the funds in higher-yielding instruments, such as the South Africa rand and U.S. stocks and bonds. \par \par \uc2  Political instability in Italy and a rise in Japanese money market interest rates also forced them to review their strategy and buy back yen to repay the funds. The Japanese rate rise was spurred by a recent comment by Japan's Finance Minister Wataru Kubo that pensioners were suffering from low interest rate policy. This raised speculation about a possible increase of Japan's official discount rate from its record-low 0.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  Until recently, U.S. hedge funds had been the main factor offsetting dollar sales by Japanese exporters, amid an absence of dollar buying by Japanese investors. Hedge fund buying helped push the dollar as high as 107.58 yen in late January. \par \par \uc2  Credit Suisse's Tanaka said there was talk that dollar long positions built by U.S. fund operators from around 80 yen last year amounted to from $30 to $70 billion. \par \par \uc2  Hiroyuki Murase, assistant vice-president at Bank of America said: "There is a possibility the dollar may fall below 100 yen by the end of March if it falls through 103 yen level." \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011019ds2m018s9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
