\par\par \b \uc2  Japan may play more active role on Asia  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Linda Sieg \par \uc2 611 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Februar 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 21 (Reuter) - Some Japanese policy-makers want Japan to play a higher-profile role in promoting Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability, but a consensus on whether it should do so has not been forged, analysts and financial sources said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo caught \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets off guard on Tuesday, when its Finance Ministry and the Bank of Japan unveiled a pact allowing Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ask counterparts in Singapore and Hong Kong to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its behalf in dollar/yen trade in their markets. \par \par \uc2  Analysts saw the move as aimed mainly at halting the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent slide against the yen to protect Japan's fragile economic recovery, but added that it was unlikely to have much lasting impact on the dollar's trend. \par \par \uc2  At least one senior Finance Ministry official, however, was keen to portray the new \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pact -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  already has one with Australia -- as part of a process of strengthening Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cooperation and promote stability. \par \par \uc2  "This is a first step towards such cooperation," a senior ministry official told reporters on Tuesday evening. \par \par \uc2  "We would try to enhance cooperation with these three countries as well as other Asian and APEC (Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation) countries in future," he said. \par \par \uc2  APEC, founded in 1989,  groups Australia, Brunei, Canada, Chile, China, Hong Kong, Indonesia, Japan, Malaysia, Mexico, New Zealand, Papua New Guinea, the Philippines, Singapore, South Korea, Taiwan, Thailand and the United States. \par \par \uc2  APEC finance ministers are to meet in Kyoto, western Japan, on March 16 and 17. \par \par \uc2  The issue of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability is on their agenda, though another Finance Ministry official said no formal proposals for an Asia-Pacific framework were likely to be made. \par \par \uc2  Still, among the issues expected to be discussed informally in Kyoto is a so-called "Asian repo" pact forged in November, a ministry source said. \par \par \uc2  "This will not be on the APEC agenda formally, but all the countries will be together so it will be discussed in the corridors," the source said. "The point would be to support regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  if they were being sold off suddenly, in the case of a crisis like that in Mexico." \par \par \uc2  In November, Australia, Hong Kong, Indonesia, Malaysia and Thailand signed the pact, allowing their central banks to borrow U.S. government securities from each other, use those reserves to bolster foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and mobilise those reserves to protect their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Japanese monetary authorities have been cautious about taking part in the repo pact, but now some in the Finance Ministry are suggesting a version that would include the United States, Japan and other Asian countries, such as Singapore and South Korea, another ministry source said. \par \par \uc2  That proposal could signal a shift in Japan's cautious stance, said C.H. Kwan, a senior economist at the Center for Policy Research at Nomura Research Institute. \par \par \uc2  "Japan will be playing a more active role in Asia in the monetary sphere," Kwan said. "There may not be a consensus, but it's moving in that direction." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said, however, that the Bank of Japan was probably still reluctant to take on the burden, similar to those shouldered elsewhere by the U.S. Federal Reserve and Germany's Bundesbank, as last resort defender of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- in part because leading Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are pegged to the dollar, not the yen. \par \par \uc2  The question of how an Asian regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  safety net would be coordinated with a global plan -- yet to be finalised -- to beef up the International Monetary Fund's emergency resources, would also need to be addressed, the analysts said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011019ds2l0179m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
