\par\par \b \uc2  Tokyo stocks recover on back of stronger dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Newland \par \uc2 1,296 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Februar 1996\par 12:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Feb 20 (Reuter) -- Tokyo stocks pulled out from a downward spiral on Tuesday after the dollar jumped more than two yen, while the few other markets open barely moved as investors waited out the Asian holiday period. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong, Jakarta, Kuala Lumpur, Seoul, Singapore, Shanghai, Shenzen and Taipei exchanges were all closed on Tuesday for Chinese New Year and Moslem holidays. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo stocks took a pounding early as the dollar's plunge overnight caused a flurry of selling. But shares then regained most of their losses after the dollar staged a spectacular rebound on Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and news that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had reached a pact on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets with Hong Kong and Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The 225-share Nikkei average ended down 65.58 points or 0.32 percent at 20,655.67, after losing more than 311 points or 1.5 percent early in the day. Turnover remained thin. \par \par \uc2  "Many investors have now retreated from the market, waiting to see New York's performance today after its long weekend," said Kazumi Shimokawa, general manager at New Japan Securities. Wall Street was closed on Monday for the President's Day holiday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was buoyed by Tokyo's announcement of a pact allowing its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ask counterparts in Singapore and Hong Kong to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's behalf. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the announcement suggested Japanese monetary authorities were strongly determined to support the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "I got a feeling that there is a really strong will in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Bank of Japan to push the dollar towards 110 yen," Makoto Kojima, director of Global Markets at Barclays Bank, told Reuters Financial Television (RFTV). \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose to above 106 yen after the announcement. \par \par \uc2  At 1119 GMT, the dollar was at 105.60/65 yen and 1.4510/15 marks, against the New York closing 104.05/15 yen and 1.4467/77 marks. \par \par \uc2  Manila shares ended lower in thin trade as foreign funds continued to stay out for the Chinese New Year holidays. \par \par \uc2  "There was a lack of foreign investors and the locals were selling blue chips to build up their cash positions for the upcoming initial public share offerings," Peter Mutuc at HG Asia Securities said. \par \par \uc2  The index shed 18.01 points to settle at 2,923.71. \par \par \uc2  Range-bound Thai stocks drifted in listless trade to end slightly lower on profit-taking and selective selling. \par \par \uc2  The SET index finished 0.66 points lower at 1,384.96. Gainers edged decliners 147 to 146 with 118 unchanged. \par \par \uc2  "It's still Chinese New Year. Investors can't look to other markets in the region for direction and foreigners didn't do much at all," a Dhana Siam broker said. \par \par \uc2  Blue chip shares dragged the Bombay Stock Exchange index down 34.53 points at the close in dull trade. \par \par \uc2  Traders said activity was hit by poor foreign buying because of Chinese New Year holidays in Hong Kong and Singapore where many of the funds are based. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong gold market was closed on Tuesday for the Chinese New Year holidays and will reopen on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  In Tokyo, spot gold was quoted at $405.00/405.40 per ounce at the close, against $405.30/405.80 in London on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Crude oil prices were slow in late afternoon Tokyo trade with most of the market on holiday for the lunar new year which ends on Wednesday, traders said. \par \par \uc2  April North Sea Brent was assessed nominally at $17.20/17.25 in late afternoon Tokyo, little changed from the late Monday London level. \par \par \uc2  SIMEX Brent was untraded throughout the day with the April contract quoted at $17.25/17.28. \par \par \uc2  Following are the Asian market highlights: \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -  Tokyo stocks took a pounding early on Tuesday as the dollar's plunge overnight caused a flurry of selling. But shares then regained most of their losses after the dollar staged a spectacular rebound on Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and news of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pact with Asian nations. \par \par \uc2  The 225-share Nikkei average ended down 65.58 points or 0.32 percent at 20,655.67, after losing more than 311 points or 1.5 percent early in the day. Turnover remained thin. \par \par \uc2  "Many investors have now retreated from the market, waiting to see New York's performance today after its long weekend," said Kazumi Shimokawa, general manager at New Japan Securities. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG - Market closed Monday, reopening February 22. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK -  Range-bound Thai stocks drifted in listless Lunar new year trade to end slightly lower on profit-taking and selective selling, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The SET index finished 0.66 points lower at 1,384.96. Gainers edged decliners 147 to 146 with 118 unchanged. \par \par \uc2  "It's still Chinese New Year, investors can't look to other markets in the region for direction and foreigners didn't do much at all," a Dhana Siam broker said. \par \par \uc2  "We saw locals buying in the morning and selling in the afternoon," he said. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY -  Blue chip shares closed lower in lacklustre trade on the Bombay Stock Exchange (BSE), undermined by insignificant foreign demand, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The 30-share BSE index dropped 34.53 points, or 0.99 percent, to a provisional 3,453.80. \par \par \uc2  Traders said foreign support was poor because of the Chinese New Year holiday in Hong Kong and Singapore where many funds are based. \par \par \uc2  - - - \par \par \uc2  COLOMBO - Sri Lanka stocks closed soft but turnover was propped up by overseas buying of blue chip Aitken Spence, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The Colombo Stock Exchange all share index cosed 1.92 points lower at 655.33. \par \par \uc2  "Even though Hong Kong is on holiday, you can contact fund managers at home if you have a good quantity of recommended stock," said a broker. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -  Closed, trading resuming on February 26. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR - Closed, reopening on February 26. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  MANILA - Manila shares ended lower in thin trade as foreign funds continued to stay out for the Chinese New Year holidays, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  "There was a lack of foreign investors and the locals were selling blue chips to build up their cash positions for the upcoming initial public share offerings," Peter Mutuc at HG Asia Securities said. \par \par \uc2  The index shed 18.01 points, or 0.61 percent, to settle at 2,923.71. Traders said investors bailed out of most blue chips to snap up secondliner stocks. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SEOUL - Closed, resuming trading on February 21. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, SHENZHEN - Closed until March 4. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE - Closed, reopening February 22. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY - The Australian share market ended lower again on Tuesday, despite putting up an early fight, as futures fell away in the afternoon session. \par \par \uc2  At the close, the All Ordinaries index was 20.0 points lower at 2,245.5. \par \par \uc2  The March share price index was 10.0 points lower at 2,256 and at a 10.5 point premium to the underlying index. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said investors had become nervous in the afternoon session with investors selling out before Wall Street reopens after the President's Day holiday in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  "Some people were a bit nervous with New York closed and that saw some selling in the afternoon," said Jim Tredenick, senior dealer at brokerage Nevitts Ltd. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  TAIPEI -  Closed, reopening Friday, February 23. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  WELLINGTON - The New Zealand share market shrugged off the negative sentiment that had dogged it for most of the day to close a touch firmer. \par \par \uc2  The NZSE-40 Capital Index ended up 2.37 points at 2,091.03 after market leader Telecom clawed its way back into the positive to end up a cent on NZ$6.44. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said Telecom accounted for $18 million of the total $46 million turnover. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011019ds2k0169c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
