\par\par \b \uc2  JGB futures down on yen, oversupply by close. \b0\par\par\uc2 439 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Februar 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 20 (Reuter) - Key March Japanese government bond futures and the June JGB futures contract, which is expected to take over the benchmark status on Thursday, ended down on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Concerns of an oversupply of bonds weighed on the market with a spate of straight bond launches expected on Tuesday and a 10-year JGB auction planned for Wednesday, dealers said. The yen's fall also reinforced the bearish market sentiment. \par \par \uc2  The March ended at 119.00, down 0.39 from Monday, on volume of 30,131 contracts. The June ended 0.44 down at 117.50 on turnover of 52,830 contracts. \par \par \uc2  The June contract exceeded the March in volume for the first time. If the June's turnover continues to outnumber that of the March on Wednesday, the benchmark status will officially be transfered to the June contract. \par \par \uc2  The primary market saw two jumbo launches: Nippon Telegraph and Telephone's 100 billion yen worth of 10-year bonds and East Japan Railway's East Japan Railway Co's 100 billion yen worth of 20-year bonds later in the day. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  also said it would offer 10-year JGBs through a price-competitive auction, for which the market was expecting an offer of 1.2 trillion yen worth of JGBs. \par \par \uc2  But there was a rumor in the market that the MOF's offer amount could be about one trillion yen, after the market was oversupplied with so many straight corporate bonds offered on Tuesday, one securities firm dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The market expects the coupon rate on the JGBs to be auctioned on Wednesday to be 3.2 or 3.3 percent. \par \par \uc2  The yen's fall added to bad sentiment in the market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yen fell by more than two yen against the dollar by afternoon in Tokyo, hit by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's announcement of an Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pact. \par \par \uc2  While some said the de facto benchmark June contract may test 118, others said it could slip further. \par \par \uc2  "The market started to feel afraid of higher interest rates in the future," said another securities firm dealer. \par \par \uc2  "If the June falls below its recent low of 117.27, the whole picture has to change," said one analyst at a securities firm. \par \par \uc2  In the cash bond market, the benchmark 174th JGBs were yielding 3.080 percent, up 0.030 from Monday, as of 0747 GMT. Cash bonds were weak, but the longer end fared better, dealers said. -- Hideyuki Sano, Tokyo Treasury Desk, (81-3-3432-8586) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011019ds2k0165g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
