\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar recovers in Tokyo, but energy seen lacking. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tatsuo Ito \par \uc2 639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Februar 1996\par 10:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 7 (Reuter) - The dollar bounced back from a sudden slide in Tokyo on Wednesday, but dealers said they had little confidence in it having the energy to climb further for the time being. \par \par \uc2  "There is a feeling that the dollar has mostly confirmed its near-term bottom, although the process of adjustment looks a bit insufficient," said Yasuhisa Morikuni, assistant adviser at the Bank of America in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The dollar dived to 104.65 yen in early Asian trade as bearish sentiment mounted due to market frustration at its failure to top its recent high of 107.58 yen, hit on January 31 in Tokyo. But fears about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and brisk bargain-hunting perked it up. \par \par \uc2  "We had brisk buying from importers and the lack of follow-through sales after early speculative sales also helped push the dollar back quickly," said Bank of America's Morikuni. \par \par \uc2  Some Japanese investors and overseas funds also moved to snap up dollars, which squeezed short positions and accelerated the dollar's gains, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The recent falls were part of position adjustments, and given the absence of dollar-selling factors and an upbeat U.S. stock market, the downside risks were proved limited," said Shinichi Abe, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nikko Securities Co. \par \par \uc2  Wall Street on Tuesday extended its run into record territory, with the blue-chip Dow Jones industrial average climbing 52.02 points to end at 5,459.61. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 105.85 yen and 1.4752 marks in late Tokyo trade, compared with 105.20 yen and 1.4715 marks in late New York on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo dealers said unconfirmed talk that the Bank of Japan (BOJ) had checked the dollar/yen rate at around 104.70-80 yen was behind the abrupt morning rally. \par \par \uc2  A rate check with several commercial banks is regarded as a possible preliminary to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been refraining from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market for the past several months after a heavy volume of dollar purchases earlier in 1995. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen to be wary that the dollar could fall below 104 yen, as such a sharp fall would completely wipe out all the gains made at the start of this year," said a European bank dealer in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  In addition, while the dollar managed to recover against the mark and the Swiss franc overnight after recent losses, it showed itself more vulnerable to falls against the yen than other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early Asian trade. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said a clear break down through 104 yen would have opened the way for the dollar to tumble to 102 yen. \par \par \uc2  "It is no wonder that the BOJ steps in to buy dollars on falls to demonstrate its coordinated stance with other nations calling for a stronger dollar," said Kazunori Iida, a manager at Nissho Iwai Corp. \par \par \uc2  Many dealers said the dollar was unlikely to break through this year's high of 107.58 yen soon because of remaining long liquidation by overseas funds and sporadic sales by Japanese exporters, expecting the dollar to hold in a 104-107 yen range in the near term. \par \par \uc2  Institutions' book closings at the end of Japan's fiscal year on March 31 and liquidations of overseas units of Japanese companies may also limit the dollar's advance, they said. \par \par \uc2  Toyomitsu Sakata, head of Treasury sales at National Westminster Bank in Tokyo, said that before the dollar could test the upside, it would be necessary to confirm whether Japanese investors would seriously move to buy it on falls. \par \par \uc2  Such dollar buying could show that there are still more buyers than sellers of the dollar in the market, he added. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011019ds2701tks\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
